\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1036\langfe1036\cgrid\langnp1036\langfenp1036 {\b\f1\fs22\lang2057\langfe1036\langnp2057                                                 PROXY VOTING
\par 
\par As from September 2005 the Privy Council have endorsed the Single Membership Charter Motion.  
This now entitles all Members to vote and, if elected or appointed, to hold office or be a member of any Committee, provided that their Branch subscription is no more than 3 months in arrears.  
Therefore, at Branch Annual General Meetings now being held, all Members may vote and may be nominated for Officer or Committee posts at all levels.  In the U.K. RBL rules do not provide for proxy voting, but the Charity rules of the French Loi 1901, whi
ch apply to the Paris Branch, may supersede the RBL ruling.  
In the case of the Paris Branch, where many members live some considerable distance from Paris, and who cannot attend the relevant meetings, such members may arrange for their proxy vote to be held by another member.  
A member so wishing to arrange for a proxy must personally obtain the approval of another member who is prepared to act as proxy, and then ensure that the duly completed proxy form is in the hands of his/her proxy by the date of the meeting.  
Members may hold no more than two proxies each.
